Lab: Test Traffic Mirroring for Dark Launches
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Explain the concept of traffic mirroring and its role in safe dark launches
Build a containerized environment that duplicates live traffic to a staging service without affecting production responses
Use monitoring and scripted validation to confirm production isolation while a new feature is tested in staging
Analyze response-time and success-rate data to compare production and staging performance
Troubleshoot common traffic-mirroring configuration issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic Linux command-line skills
Familiarity with Docker and Docker Compose
Basic understanding of HTTP, ports, and reverse proxies
Basic Python familiarity (reading async code, not writing it from scratch)
This lab assumes a clean Ubuntu 22.04 LTS machine (or equivalent) with Docker and Docker Compose already available. If they are not installed, install them and the remaining dependencies with:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y docker.io docker-compose-plugin python3-pip jq curl
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
pip3 install --user aiohttp
Log out and back in (or run newgrp docker) after the usermod command so your shell picks up Docker group permissions.

All work in this lab happens inside your own isolated practice environment. You are only mirroring synthetic traffic that you generate yourself against containers you own; no external systems or real user data are involved.

